  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 315 PM EDT FRI OCT 16 2026\n\nThe long-term forecast period opens Sunday morning with 500 hPa\nridging over the Central CONUS between an ~574 dam low off the \nCarolina Coast and a ~578 dam low over the Lower Colorado River \nBasin. The ridging aloft will support strong ~1038 mb surface\nhigh pressure situated over the Mid-Atlantic with a ridge axis \nextending southwest all the way into Texas. The only other feature\nof note will be a coastal low churning up rough seas off of the \nCarolinas.\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern will largely hold in place through the\nduration of the long-term period -- though some of the upper- \nlevel features will become more amplified with time. The surface \nhigh center will also propagate northward but all the while \nmaintain a cool and dry north to northeasterly low-level flow into\neastern Kentucky. This will keep the air mass over eastern\nKentucky slow to modify through the work week. In fact, the \ncombination of dry air and light winds should again favor \nsignificant frost or a light freeze on Sunday night into Monday \nmorning in the valleys. While successive nights will gradually \nturn milder, the pattern remains favorable for renewed light frost\nformation on Monday and Tuesday nights in the typically colder \nspots.\n\nIn sensible terms, expect fair weather and light winds from Sunday \nthrough Friday along with a slow warming trend. Daily high temperatures\nstart the week in the upper 50s to mid 60s on Sunday and moderate\nto the lower to middle 70s by Friday. Meanwhile, nighttime lows \nrange in the 30s on Sunday night with extensive frost probable in \nvalleys, but slowly moderate into mostly the 40s by Thursday night\ninto Friday morning. Patchy frost appears possible in the coldest\nnorthern hollows through at least Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning.",
